==============================================================================
FINAL ANSWER: why the (-2,3) filling forces tr(B)=tr(Abb)
==============================================================================
The locus {relator=I, tr(B)=tr(Abb)} -- imposed on the bare group
presentation, with NO filling assumed -- splits into exactly two
irreducible components:
  (1) a component IDENTICAL, generator-for-generator, to the full
      (-2,3)-filled character variety (all 4 Galois conjugates);
  (2) a degree-2 component consisting entirely of REDUCIBLE
      representations (the generic degenerate/abelian-image branch
      every one-relator group's character variety carries).

So tr(B)=tr(Abb) is not a coincidence discovered AT (-2,3) -- among
IRREDUCIBLE representations, it is an exact algebraic
CHARACTERIZATION of the (-2,3) Dehn filling itself: the filling
relation, reduced using the group relator, is exactly equivalent,
as a condition on the representation, to forcing tr(B)=tr(Abb).

